    "faq": {
      "type": "bella-faq",
      "blocks": {
        "q1": {
          "type": "qa",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Does this meet California's disclosure rules?",
            "answer": "Yes. AB 723 requires a statement on or next to each digitally altered listing image, plus access to the original photo — posted beside it or through a link. LA orders ship with disclosure wording; keep your original photos to post or link next to the staged set. Your broker or MLS is the final word on placement."
          }
        },
        "q2": {
          "type": "qa",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Can you stage the patio and yard, not just inside?",
            "answer": "Yes, and in LA we'd push for it. Indoor-outdoor space is what the market is buying, and staged patios and decks routinely draw more engagement than staged living rooms."
          }
        },
        "q3": {
          "type": "qa",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Can you turn my daytime exterior into a twilight shot?",
            "answer": "Yes — that's day-to-dusk editing, and it's one of the most-ordered add-ons on LA listings. A flat 2pm exterior becomes the golden-hour hero shot without a second photography visit."
          }
        },
        "q4": {
          "type": "qa",
          "settings": {
            "question": "My listing is a period Spanish. Will the furniture look wrong?",
            "answer": "It shouldn't — a designer composes to the architecture rather than dropping in a template. Spanish revival, mid-century and new-build spec homes each get a different furniture vocabulary."
          }
        },
        "q5": {
          "type": "qa",
          "settings": {
            "question": "How much does virtual staging cost in Los Angeles?",
            "answer": "Prices vary by provider and by whether a person or an AI app does the work. Bella's designer-made virtual staging is {price:virtual-staging} per photo, with volume discounts of 5% at 3+ photos, 10% at 6+, 15% at 11+ and 20% at 21+. Furniture removal for occupied rooms, unlimited revisions for two weeks and full usage rights are included — no subscription, no minimum."
          }
        },
        "q6": {
          "type": "qa",
          "settings": {
            "question": "How much does home staging cost in Los Angeles?",
            "answer": "Physical staging in Los Angeles is quoted per home by the stager — size, number of rooms and rental length all move the price. As a general benchmark, conventional staging runs about $2,000–5,000 a month until the home sells. Virtual staging from Bella is {price:virtual-staging} a photo, paid once, with no rental to extend if the listing sits."
          }
        },
        "q7": {
          "type": "qa",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Is virtual staging worth it?",
            "answer": "For most vacant or dated listings, yes. Staging can't guarantee a sale, but 97% of buyers use the internet in their home search, according to the National Association of Realtors, so the photos decide which homes get a showing. NAR's research on home staging also finds that staging makes it easier for buyers to visualize a property as their future home. In Los Angeles, where the lifestyle is the product, staged photos show buyers how the home lives for a one-time cost per photo instead of monthly furniture rental."
          }
        },
        "q8": {
          "type": "qa",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Is virtual staging legal in Los Angeles?",
            "answer": "Yes, as long as it isn't misleading. Under AB 723 (California Business and Professions Code §10140.8), a digitally altered listing image needs a conspicuous statement on or next to it saying it was altered, plus access to the original, unaltered image — posted alongside it or through a link or QR code. The requirement sits in the state's Real Estate Law, where a violation is a crime, so it's worth getting right. Add furniture and decor only; don't hide defects or change permanent features. Every Bella order includes disclosure guidance, and your broker or MLS has the final say on placement."
          }
        },
        "q9": {
          "type": "qa",
          "settings": {
            "question": "What are common virtual staging mistakes?",
            "answer": "Oversized or unrealistic furniture that misrepresents the room, hiding defects or changing permanent features, a design style that doesn't fit the buyer, low-resolution original photos (start with high-quality, well-lit shots), and failing to disclose virtual staging. In California, forgetting to provide the original photo with a digitally altered image is also an AB 723 problem."
          }
        },
        "q10": {
          "type": "qa",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Should I use DIY software or a virtual staging company?",
            "answer": "DIY software and AI apps are cheap and instant, but you do the design work and results can distort scale. A done-for-you service like Bella assigns a real designer, removes existing furniture and includes revisions — a better fit for luxury listings, where buyers compare the photos with the real room."
          }
        },
        "q11": {
          "type": "qa",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Can you show a room in more than one style, or a home that isn't built yet?",
            "answer": "Yes. Because the furniture is digital, a room can be staged in more than one Look Book style, which makes it easy to test which look suits the buyer. For dated rooms, virtual renovation (from {price:virtual-renovation-service}) shows new floors, walls and finishes; for homes that aren't built yet, 3D rendering (from {price:3d-rendering}) creates photorealistic images from plans."
          }
        },
        "q12": {
          "type": "qa",
          "settings": {
            "question": "How can I virtually stage my home for free?",
            "answer": "Free AI apps and DIY software can drop furniture into a photo, but the results often distort scale and finishes, and a misleading image creates disclosure problems. New clients can claim a $40 voucher on Bella's sign-up page to try designer-made staging, subject to Bella's approval."
          }
        }
      },
